From 48618e04281ed6e60c11725cf8c6597db7d1a66b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dennis Berger <theabyssinspace@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 09:45:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] refactor(core): Extract the execution loop into
 StackScheduler (no-changelog)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`WorkflowExecute.processRunExecutionData()` held the ~490-line `executionLoop:`
inline: pop the next node from `nodeExecutionStack`, run it with retries, record
its task data, queue its successors and, once the stack is empty, run the
multi-input nodes still waiting for some of their inputs. This moves that loop
verbatim into `StackScheduler`, behind two small interfaces in
`workflow-scheduler.ts`:

- `WorkflowScheduler`: `run(host, workflow, runExecutionData, hooks)` plus the
  two values the completion chain reads afterwards, `executionError` and
  `closeFunction` (the latter also on the failure path, to deactivate the
  trigger).
- `SchedulerHost`: the 30 members of `WorkflowExecute` the loop used (27
  methods, `additionalData`, `mode` and a new `abortSignal` getter), as a `Pick`
  so the class stays the single definition of their signatures.

The 22 private helpers the loop called are now public so the scheduler can reach
them. `runExecutionData` stays private (the tests pin that) and is passed to
`run()` instead. Everything around the loop — setup, the issue check, the
waiting state, cancellation and the `.then/.catch/.finally` chain — is
unchanged, and so is behaviour: the execution-engine suite produces the same
1657 results.

`planEngineRequest()` is the one member that is new rather than moved. It is
`handleEngineRequest()` without the `addNodeToBeExecuted()` calls: the plan
`handleRequest()` produces, returned instead of pushed onto
`nodeExecutionStack`. A scheduler that keeps its own representation of pending
work needs n8n to *build* an agent's tool round — the reserved run-data slots,
the `rewireOutputLogTo` tag, the source-overwrite metadata — without deciding
when any of it runs. `handleEngineRequest()` is unchanged and still what
`StackScheduler` calls.
